#
# The router's daily look at what it could install (ADR-014). The crontab line
# that runs this is package content: it never varies per device and is never
# edited at runtime, because whether the check happens at all is one uci option.
# Turning it off is therefore a setting an owner changes in Verso or with one
# `uci set` over SSH, and never a crontab edit that the next package upgrade
# would fight. An explicit 1 is the only value that means yes: a bare config
# never reaches the network on its own.
#
# Identical crontabs across a fleet would hit the package feeds and the
# sysupgrade server on the same minute, so a random offset of up to an hour
# precedes the check. --now skips it, for a person at a prompt who wants the
# answer rather than a fleet's good manners.
#
# The check itself is the Verso binary's one-shot mode: it asks the privileged
# helper the two read verbs under rpcd's local-root session and renames the
# result into /var/run/verso/state, where every Verso surface reads it. Nothing
# in this path installs, applies, or upgrades anything.
[ "$(uci -q get verso.updates.autocheck)" = "1" ] || exit 0
# hexdump is a busybox applet a trimmed build can omit; if it is missing the
# substitution is empty and the offset falls back to zero, so the check still
# runs — a fleet losing its spread beats the check dying silently.
if [ "$1" != "--now" ]; then
	offset="$(hexdump -n2 -e '/2 "%u"' /dev/urandom 2>/dev/null)"
	sleep "$(( ${offset:-0} % 3600 ))"
fi
exec /usr/bin/verso update-check
